<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1632">1632</a>–<a href="/years/1723">1723</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonie van Leeuwenhoek was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> draper and self-taught scientist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>. Using small single-lens <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">microscopes</a> of his own making, he was the first to observe and describe bacteria and protozoa, and he studied sperm cells and the flow of blood in capillaries, reporting his discoveries in letters to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society of London</a> from 1673. He is regarded as the father of microbiology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonie-van-leeuwenhoek.jpg" alt="Antonie van Leeuwenhoek" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Verkolje · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthonie_van_Leeuwenhoek_(1632-1723)._Natuurkundige_te_Delft_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-957.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antonie van Leeuwenhoek was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a> on 24 October 1632. His father, a basket maker, died when Antonie was five, and his mother came from a well-to-do brewer's family. At sixteen he became a bookkeeper's apprentice at a linen-draper's shop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. In July 1654 he married Barbara de Mey in Delft and opened a draper's shop. In 1660 he became chamberlain for the sheriffs in the city hall, a post he held for almost 40 years, and in 1669 he was appointed land surveyor by the court of Holland. He knew the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-vermeer">Johannes Vermeer</a>, whose will he executed in 1675. He was Dutch Reformed and Calvinist, and believed his discoveries were further proof of the wonder of creation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Wanting to see the thread of his cloth better than the magnifying lenses of the time allowed, Van Leeuwenhoek took up lensmaking. A small glass rod pulled apart in a flame yields two whiskers of glass, and putting the end of one back in the flame forms a very small, high-quality lens. He kept his methods secret. He made more than 500 lenses and at least 25 single-lens <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">microscopes</a>, of which nine survive and magnify up to 275 times.</p><p>His friend, the physician Reinier de Graaf, introduced his work to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, which in 1673 published a letter from him on mold, bees and lice. In 1674 he concluded that the moving objects under his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">microscope</a> were little animals. His report of single-celled organisms, dated 9 October 1676, was met with scepticism until observers sent by the Royal Society confirmed his observations in 1677. He was elected to the Society in February 1680 but never attended a meeting, and he wrote only in colloquial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. Visitors included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>, though he refused to show them his best microscopes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He was the first to recognize that the structures Marcello Malpighi and Jan Swammerdam had seen in blood were red blood cells. His records include protists in 1674, spermatozoa in 1677, the banded pattern of muscle fibres in 1682 and bacteria in 1683. He also experimented on himself, examining blood drawn from his finger and his own skin. He died on 26 August 1723, aged 90, and was buried in the Oude Kerk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antonie van Leeuwenhoek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Relative freedom of the press made Leiden and Amsterdam centres of European publishing, where René Descartes, Baruch Spinoza and John Locke lived and wrote, while Christiaan Huygens, <b>Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</b> and the Leiden medical school of Herman Boerhaave advanced science.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hooke’s Micrographia (1665) and the letters of <b>Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</b> from Delft opened the world of the microscope; Christiaan Huygens designed the first pendulum clock and explained the ring of Saturn; Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz developed the calculus independently of Newton and published it first, in 1684.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch Republic, with about 1.5 million people, became a great power in the 17th century and controlled a worldwide network of trade; its relative religious toleration drew refugees, merchants and thinkers, and its Golden Age produced Rembrandt, Johannes Vermeer, Hugo Grotius, Christiaan Huygens and <b>Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch lawyers such as Hugo Grotius led in international and commercial law, and <b>Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</b> was the first to describe bacteria.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.01,4.36&amp;year=1691" aria-label="Delft on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4648 366 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antonie van Leeuwenhoek"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5098 636h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5098 636h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.01,4.36&amp;year=1691">What was here in 1691? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
